"""Editorial lessons for the illustrated How Tax Moves series."""
# Each lesson has its own mechanism and example; group controls the drawing legend.
SERIES=[
('C-Corp Planning','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p542','corporation',[
('How does C corporation income tax work before an owner takes cash?', 'The corporation computes its own taxable income and generally pays federal corporate income tax at 21%, with state and other applicable rules reviewed separately. An owner does not automatically report all corporate operating profit on an individual return. Taking money out later has its own wage, dividend, loan or other classification.','A corporation earns $100,000 of taxable income. A simplified federal illustration gives $21,000 of corporate tax and $79,000 after that tax, before state taxes and any owner payment.'),
('How does a C corporation dividend create a second layer of tax?', 'A dividend generally comes from after-tax corporate earnings and is not a corporate deduction. The shareholder applies the dividend rules on the personal return, including qualification and any applicable additional tax. This is why the corporate rate alone cannot represent the owner\'s eventual combined burden.','Follow $100,000 of taxable profit through the corporate return, then follow the after-tax amount into an owner dividend. The drawing separates the two tax events.'),
('How does retaining earnings in a C corporation work?', 'Retaining cash leaves it inside the company for company purposes. Corporate tax can still apply to taxable profit; cash retention does not make the earnings untaxed. Consider business needs and accumulated earnings and personal holding company rules where relevant before assuming indefinite retention solves owner tax.','The company keeps cash to expand operations instead of paying a dividend. That reserve decision occurs after calculating the corporate tax position.'),
('How does owner payroll reduce C corporation taxable profit?', 'Reasonable compensation for actual services can generally be a corporate business expense, subject to applicable rules. The owner reports wages, and the employer and employee payroll obligations remain. Compare the corporation\'s deduction with the employee\'s income and both sides of employment tax.','A company pays an owner for management work. The same payment appears as a potential wage expense to the employer and wage income to the employee.'),
('How does a C corporation capital contribution differ from revenue?', 'A shareholder investment is generally capital funding, not customer income. It belongs in the ownership and basis records rather than the sales ledger. Identify whether money is equity, a bona fide loan or another transaction before classifying it. Property contributions can have additional tax consequences.','An owner deposits money to fund startup operations. The bank deposit is not automatically a sale or a deductible expense.'),
('How does buying equipment with C corporation cash work?', 'The purchase exchanges cash for an asset. A current deduction depends on depreciation rules, eligible expensing, service date and use. Spending money is not by itself the same as reducing taxable income by the entire price. Model future recapture and state adjustments as well.','The company buys a machine and records it in the asset register. The drawing follows cash separately from the later depreciation deduction.'),
('How does a corporate loan to a shareholder work?', 'A genuine loan creates a receivable for the corporation and a debt obligation for the shareholder. Repayment intent, terms, interest and actual conduct must support the loan. Personal spending or a bookkeeping label alone can lead to compensation or distribution treatment instead.','The company advances cash under a documented repayment agreement. Principal, interest and eventual repayment are separate flows, not an automatic business deduction.'),
('How does reasonable business accumulation differ from a personal investment reserve?', 'Corporate cash should be considered in light of actual business needs and applicable accumulation rules. Specific expansion or working-capital plans support a different explanation from retaining money solely to avoid shareholder tax. Document plans and review investment income and ownership when additional corporate taxes could apply.','The board approves a defined expansion budget. That business plan is more informative than an unexplained large cash or securities balance.'),
('How does a C corporation loss affect the shareholder return?', 'A C corporation operating loss generally stays with the corporation rather than passing through as the shareholder\'s personal deduction. Corporate loss rules and shareholder investment loss rules are distinct. Ownership of the company does not make its annual business loss a Schedule E offset.','The company reports a loss while the owner has unrelated wages. The drawing keeps the corporate result on its own return.'),
('How does moving from C corporation tax treatment to S treatment work?', 'An S election changes the federal framework if eligibility and procedure requirements are met, but prior C corporation history can still matter. Review built-in gains, accumulated earnings and profits, ownership and state treatment before electing. The election is not a retroactive erasure of corporate tax history.','A profitable company with appreciated assets considers an election. Compare the transition rules before relying on pass-through treatment.'),
]),
('S-Corp Planning','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/s-corporation-stock-and-debt-basis','pass-through',[
('How does S corporation profit reach an owner\'s personal return?', 'The corporation reports business items and allocates them to shareholders on Schedule K-1. Owners then report applicable items on their individual returns, subject to relevant limitations. A cash distribution is a separate event; retaining corporate cash does not generally stop pass-through income.','The company has $100,000 of allocated profit but distributes only $40,000. The reporting arrow represents income, not the amount withdrawn.'),
('How does S corporation payroll differ from a shareholder distribution?', 'Payroll pays for shareholder services and produces wages and employment-tax reporting. A distribution is an ownership payment with its own basis rules. Reasonable compensation must be addressed before relying on nonwage transfers for a shareholder who works in the business.','A working owner receives wages through payroll and a separate approved distribution. The drawing uses distinct paths because the classifications differ.'),
('How does stock basis change when an S corporation earns income?', 'Allocated income generally increases stock basis, while distributions and other adjustments can reduce it. The calculation is shareholder-specific and follows applicable ordering rules. Corporate bank cash and book equity are not substitutes for the owner\'s tax basis schedule.','Start with opening stock basis, add allocated income and review distributions. Keep losses and nondeductible items visible in the reconciliation.'),
('How does an S corporation distribution above stock basis work?', 'A nondividend distribution is generally tax-free only to the extent of stock basis; an excess can create gain. Debt basis does not automatically shelter a distribution. Prior C corporation earnings and profits can introduce additional rules, so verify the company\'s history.','An owner proposes a withdrawal larger than their stock basis. Calculate the tax effect before making the transfer.'),
('How does an S corporation loss move through owner limitations?', 'Allocated losses must pass through stock and debt basis, at-risk, passive activity and excess business loss rules as applicable. The K-1 starts the analysis rather than approving the deduction. Keep suspensions separate because their release conditions differ.','A large depreciation loss reaches the shareholder file. The drawing shows a review gate before any current personal deduction.'),
('How does shareholder funding support an S corporation expense?', 'An owner can contribute capital or make a bona fide direct loan, and the company can use the cash for actual operations. Funding is not itself an expense; the business must classify what it buys. The owner\'s basis effect is separate from the company\'s expense treatment.','The shareholder funds payroll during a slow month. The funding and the wage expense belong to different parts of the records.'),
('How does an S corporation health insurance reimbursement reach the W-2?', 'More-than-2% shareholder premiums paid or reimbursed by the corporation have special wage and deduction treatment. Verify establishment of the arrangement, Box 1 reporting and the shareholder\'s eligibility for the separate deduction. Do not treat the payment like an ordinary rank-and-file employee benefit without review.','The corporation reimburses documented premiums and coordinates payroll reporting. The drawing distinguishes the payment from the shareholder deduction analysis.'),
('How does one owner\'s S corporation loan differ from another owner\'s basis?', 'A genuine direct shareholder loan can create debt basis for the lending shareholder under applicable rules. It does not automatically give the other shareholders the same basis. Track each investment and loan relationship individually rather than allocating all funding by ownership percentage.','One of two owners funds the company. Review the actual lender relationship instead of dividing the debt basis equally.'),
('How does a shareholder tax distribution differ from company tax?', 'An S corporation may distribute cash to help owners pay tax on allocated income. The payment generally remains a shareholder distribution and does not become a corporate income-tax deduction simply because the owner uses it for tax. State entity taxes require separate review.','The company sends cash for an owner\'s estimated payment. The corporate transfer and the individual IRS payment are two transactions.'),
('How does a shareholder loan repayment differ from an S corporation distribution?', 'A genuine loan repayment reduces the company debt and must be compared with the shareholder\'s adjusted debt basis. A distribution uses stock-basis rules. Prior loss deductions can make repayment taxable, so the payment label needs to match the actual obligation and tax history.','The company repays an old owner loan after profitable operations. Reconcile the reduced loan basis before assuming the repayment is entirely tax-free.'),
]),
('Entity Structuring','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p541','partnership',[
('How does partnership income move from Form 1065 to a partner?', 'A partnership generally files an information return and allocates items on Schedule K-1. The partner reports the applicable share, with character and limitations preserved as required. Cash distributions need not equal taxable allocations, which makes both schedules important.','Two partners receive K-1 income even though most cash stays in the partnership. The information arrows and cash arrows represent different events.'),
('How does a partnership cash distribution differ from a profit allocation?', 'A profit allocation determines the partner\'s share of taxable items under the governing rules. A distribution moves cash or property and affects basis, with potential gain when cash exceeds outside basis. The agreement must be evaluated with the tax rules rather than treating either number as interchangeable.','A partner receives little cash but has taxable income. The distribution amount does not cap the allocated income.'),
('How does a guaranteed payment to a partner work?', 'A qualifying guaranteed payment is determined without regard to partnership income. Its reporting and deduction or capitalization depend on the facts. Partners generally are not employees of their partnership, so this should not be processed as ordinary partner W-2 payroll.','A partnership compensates a partner for ongoing services under a fixed arrangement. Separate that payment from the partner\'s share of residual profit.'),
('How does a partner capital contribution affect the partnership?', 'Cash or property contributed as capital funds the partnership and generally affects the contributing partner\'s outside basis. The partnership\'s property basis and capital-account records have separate rules. Debt and built-in gain can change the analysis for property contributions.','A new partner contributes cash and another contributes appreciated property. The two contributions require more than matching bank values.'),
('How does partnership debt affect a partner\'s basis?', 'A partner\'s share of qualifying partnership liabilities can affect outside basis. Liability type and allocation rules matter, and basis is not the same as being economically at risk. A change in liabilities can also have deemed contribution or distribution effects.','The partnership refinances a property. Review each partner\'s liability share and basis changes instead of recording only the new loan balance.'),
('How does a partnership loss reach a partner\'s tax return?', 'The partnership allocates the loss, but the partner must apply outside-basis, at-risk, passive and other applicable limitations. Tax capital alone does not establish deductibility. The partner file should track each suspended category and the conditions for future use.','A property partnership generates a depreciation loss. The K-1 does not establish that every partner can offset wages.'),
('How does a partnership tax distribution help owners pay tax?', 'The partnership can distribute cash under its governing agreement to fund partners\' tax obligations. Taxable allocations and distribution treatment remain separate. Review each partner\'s situation and the cash policy; a payment for taxes is not automatically a deductible partnership expense.','The partnership retains cash for improvements but provides a tax distribution. Show the individual tax payment as a separate step.'),
('How does a rental partnership with unequal owner work hours operate for tax?', 'The partnership allocates economic items under applicable rules, while material participation is generally evaluated for each owner. One partner\'s active work does not automatically make an unrelated partner active. Ownership percentage and participation answer different questions.','One partner manages guests and another supplies capital. Keep allocation records separate from each owner\'s participation evidence.'),
('How does a partner reimbursement differ from a guaranteed payment?', 'A reimbursement accounts for actual partnership business costs paid by a partner, while a guaranteed payment compensates under its own rules. Agreement terms, substantiation and reporting matter. Do not relabel a fixed service payment as reimbursement without an underlying expense.','A partner submits an itemized invoice for partnership supplies. Review the reimbursement separately from the regular service compensation.'),
('How does buying a partnership interest differ from buying its property?', 'An interest purchase creates outside basis for the buyer, while the partnership owns its assets with inside basis. Certain elections and adjustments can connect these layers, but they are not identical by default. Ask for a transaction-specific basis analysis before setting depreciation.','A buyer purchases an investor\'s partnership interest. The purchase price does not automatically become the property\'s new depreciation basis.'),
]),
('Tax Compliance','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15','payroll',[
('How does gross payroll split into take-home pay and tax withholding?', 'Gross wages are reduced by applicable employee taxes and other authorized deductions to arrive at net pay. The employer remits withheld amounts under deposit rules. Employer payroll taxes are an additional cost, so the bank deposit to the worker is not the full payroll expense.','A wage statement shows gross pay, withheld taxes and net pay. The drawing branches the same payroll event into worker cash and government remittances.'),
('How does the employer share of payroll tax work?', 'The employer can owe payroll taxes in addition to the employee amounts withheld from wages. Rates, wage bases and special rules depend on the tax and year. Separate employer expense from employee withholding when budgeting staff cost and reconciling payroll.','A company budgets only take-home wages. The employer tax path shows why the actual company cash cost can be higher.'),
('How does payroll tax withholding reach the IRS?', 'Amounts withheld must be deposited and reported according to the employer\'s applicable schedule. Running payroll and filing a return are not substitutes for timely deposits. Reconcile payroll registers, deposit confirmations and quarterly returns so missing remittances are visible.','The company runs payroll through a service provider. Keep deposit evidence alongside the pay register rather than relying only on an invoice.'),
('How does owner payroll work when an S corporation has one employee?', 'A single working shareholder still needs supported wage treatment and applicable employment-tax reporting. Reasonable compensation is based on actual services. The company should keep payroll separate from shareholder distributions even when the same person controls both accounts.','The sole shareholder earns customer revenue and receives wages. The drawing separates payroll reporting from ownership withdrawals.'),
('How does a bonus payment move through payroll taxes?', 'A cash bonus is generally compensation, with applicable withholding and payroll tax treatment. Supplemental-wage withholding methods do not necessarily equal the employee\'s final income-tax rate. Record the bonus as payroll rather than an unreported owner draw.','An owner approves a year-end bonus. The withholding amount and final tax liability still need to be distinguished.'),
('How does an employee 401(k) deferral come out of payroll?', 'An eligible elected deferral is withheld from compensation and sent to the plan under applicable requirements. Pretax and Roth income-tax treatment differ, and payroll tax treatment must be applied correctly. The election, payroll record and plan deposit should reconcile.','An employee elects a deferral. The drawing separates money sent to the retirement account from take-home pay.'),
('How does employee classification change the way a business pays workers?', 'The worker\'s actual relationship determines whether employee payroll or independent-contractor treatment applies. Behavioral control, financial control and the relationship matter; a contract label alone is not decisive. An employee arrangement introduces withholding and employment-tax obligations.','A business calls a full-time supervised worker a contractor. Review the actual working relationship before choosing the payment route.'),
('How does reimbursing an employee expense differ from adding wages?', 'A properly substantiated reimbursement under an accountable arrangement can be treated differently from wages. Nonaccountable payments generally need wage treatment. The underlying cost and documentation decide the route, not the convenience of adding a flat amount to each paycheck.','A worker returns unused travel advances and submits receipts. The reimbursement route differs from an unrestricted allowance.'),
('How does paying a spouse through business payroll work?', 'A spouse must perform genuine services, and compensation and payroll treatment depend on the entity and applicable family-employment rules. Record duties and actual pay. Marriage alone does not establish a deductible wage or automatic retirement-plan eligibility.','A spouse manages bookkeeping on a documented schedule. The drawing follows wages through payroll instead of treating a household transfer as employment.'),
('How does reconciling Form 941 with year-end W-2 totals work?', 'Quarterly payroll reporting and annual employee reporting should connect to the payroll records, with differences investigated and corrected appropriately. Deposits also need reconciliation. A matching year-end total does not prove every deposit was made on time.','A company compares quarterly wage reports with annual W-2 summaries and payment confirmations. The reporting path supports a complete reconciliation.'),
]),
('Entity Structuring','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/international-businesses/transfer-pricing','two-entities',[
('How does a real management fee between two businesses work?', 'One entity performs actual services for another under a supportable arrangement. The payer evaluates deduction or capitalization and related-party rules, while the recipient reports service income. Common ownership does not authorize arbitrary fees chosen simply to move taxable profit.','A shared-services company provides documented administration to an operating company. The drawing pairs the service with the invoice and income record.'),
('How does related-party pricing affect a two-entity tax plan?', 'Controlled transactions must be supportable under applicable pricing and allocation rules. Identify what was provided, who performed it and what an independent party would pay. The overall tax model should include both entities instead of showing only a payer deduction.','Two companies share an owner and set a service fee. A price supported by real work is different from a percentage selected to eliminate one company\'s profit.'),
('How does separating rental property from an operating business work?', 'Separate ownership can distinguish operating activity from property ownership, but tax classification and related-party use still matter. Rent paid by the business and reported by the property activity must be reconciled. Self-rental and other limitations can affect the owner\'s result.','The owner holds a building in one LLC and operates a business in another entity. The rent payment has a recipient as well as a payer.'),
('How does a shared payroll company charge costs to another entity?', 'Actual employees, services and cost allocation must be documented. Employment, benefit-plan, agency and related-employer rules need review before centralizing payroll. A reimbursement or service charge does not remove the underlying employer obligations or permit arbitrary deductions.','One entity employs administrative staff who support two businesses. The drawing shows a supported allocation of real costs rather than invented payroll.'),
('How does a royalty between commonly owned companies work?', 'A royalty needs genuine intellectual-property rights, actual use, supportable pricing and consistent reporting. Legal ownership and development history matter. An entity cannot create deductible royalties merely by naming itself an intellectual-property company.','An operating company uses legally documented property owned by another entity. The royalty payment and recipient income are shown together.'),
('How does a loan between two commonly owned entities work?', 'A bona fide loan creates a payable for one entity and a receivable for the other. Principal is generally a balance-sheet movement rather than a service expense. Interest, terms, repayment and related-party rules require separate analysis.','One business temporarily funds another under documented terms. The drawing keeps principal distinct from a management fee or capital contribution.'),
('How does a capital contribution to a subsidiary differ from a fee?', 'Equity funding increases the investment and capital records; it is not a deductible fee for services merely because money leaves the parent account. Classify the actual transaction and ownership relationship before recording a tax expense. Property transfers and reorganizations can introduce further rules.','A parent funds a new subsidiary\'s startup cash. Show investment funding rather than an invoice for work never performed.'),
('How does paying an invoice from the wrong business account work?', 'Identify which entity incurred the obligation and whether the other entity paid as an agent, lender or investor. Reconcile an appropriate intercompany balance and avoid deducting the same expense twice. Payment from an account does not by itself establish the taxpayer entitled to the deduction.','Company A pays a vendor bill belonging to Company B. The records should identify the actual expense and settlement between the companies.'),
('How does moving money between two LLCs owned by one person affect tax?', 'First determine whether each LLC is disregarded, a partnership or a corporation for federal tax purposes. Transfers between disregarded entities of the same owner can differ from transactions between separate taxpayers. Legal entities and bank accounts do not automatically establish two income-tax returns.','One owner has two disregarded LLCs. The drawing labels the legal cash movement while the text explains the single-owner tax framework.'),
('How does a two-entity plan change when cash must reach the owner?', 'Follow the cash through both entities and then the owner payment. A payer deduction may create recipient income, and a corporate dividend can add shareholder tax. Include wages, distributions, fees, compliance costs and state treatment before claiming the structure lowers the combined burden.','An owner needs personal cash rather than long-term company reserves. The full route matters more than one entity\'s headline tax rate.'),
]),
('Rental Property','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527','property',[
('How does rental income move from tenant payment to Schedule E?', 'Reconcile gross rent and other rental receipts with property expenses and depreciation, then apply applicable limitations on the owner return. The bank deposit is the cash event; Schedule E is the tax calculation. Entity ownership can require an entity return before the item reaches the owner.','A landlord receives rent, pays expenses and reports a result. The drawing distinguishes the property cash flow from depreciation and tax reporting.'),
('How does a mortgage payment split between principal and interest?', 'Principal reduces debt, while qualifying interest can be a rental expense under applicable rules. Escrow components and actual tax or insurance payments also need reconciliation. Treating the full mortgage debit as a deduction understates taxable income.','The lender statement separates loan reduction from interest. The drawing sends those amounts to different recordkeeping paths.'),
('How does depreciation reduce rental taxable income without spending cash?', 'Depreciation allocates qualifying property basis over time or under eligible acceleration rules. It can create a tax expense without a current cash payment. Land is excluded, and service date, recovery period and limitations matter. This explains why taxable rental results can differ from bank cash flow.','The owner pays operating expenses today and claims depreciation on an earlier property purchase. The drawing marks depreciation as a noncash tax item.'),
('How does converting a personal home to a rental affect depreciation?', 'Establish the rental service date and apply the conversion basis rules, which can use the lower of adjusted basis or fair market value for depreciation. Land allocation and personal-use history remain relevant. The original purchase price is not automatically the correct rental depreciation basis.','A homeowner moves out and makes the house available for rent. The tax file needs the conversion date and value evidence.'),
('How does a rental improvement enter the depreciation schedule?', 'A capital improvement is generally added to an asset schedule rather than deducted as an ordinary repair. Identify its cost, classification and service date. Existing asset removal can need separate review. The contractor invoice should explain the work, not just provide a total charge.','The owner replaces a major system. The drawing routes cash to the asset record before tax depreciation is calculated.'),
('How does a rental security deposit move through the books?', 'A refundable deposit is generally tracked as a liability until returned or applied under the lease. Advance rent is different. If part is retained for damages or another qualifying purpose, evaluate the income and expense treatment at that point.','A landlord holds a refundable deposit and later returns most of it. The drawing separates deposit custody from earned rent.'),
('How does personal use change a vacation rental tax calculation?', 'Personal and rental use can require expense allocation and trigger special vacation-home limitations. Maintain a day-by-day use record and classify family or below-market use correctly. A reservation platform total may omit the personal blocks that control the tax result.','The owner rents the home to guests and uses it personally. The drawing separates the two uses before deductions are computed.'),
('How does a short-term rental loss move into the household return?', 'Classify the activity, evaluate participation and apply basis, at-risk, passive and other relevant limits. Short guest stays or a cost segregation deduction do not automatically establish a current wage offset. Recordkeeping and owner-specific facts determine the path.','An STR reports a tax loss. The drawing places a limitation review between the property result and the personal deduction.'),
('How does a property manager payment affect rental reporting?', 'The manager may collect rent, deduct fees and remit net cash, but the owner must reconcile gross income and qualifying expenses. The manager\'s operating work also matters when evaluating certain participation tests. Net remittances alone do not tell the whole tax story.','A manager sends a monthly net payment. Obtain the gross rental statement and fee detail before reporting only the deposit.'),
('How does rental sale gain differ from the cash I keep at closing?', 'Tax gain generally uses amount realized and adjusted basis, while closing cash also reflects mortgage payoff and settlement items. Depreciation history affects basis and gain treatment. Prepare a tax calculation and a cash reconciliation as separate schedules.','A highly leveraged property produces little closing cash but substantial gain. The drawing shows why the mortgage payoff does not eliminate tax.'),
]),
('Retirement Planning','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p560','retirement',[
('How does an employer retirement contribution move from business to plan?', 'The employer funds the retirement account under the plan rules, with deduction timing, limits and employee requirements reviewed separately. The contribution is not an ordinary owner distribution. Confirm plan establishment, eligible compensation and actual deposit.','The business contributes cash to the plan and records the funding. The owner\'s plan account and company deduction have related but distinct rules.'),
('How does a pretax employee deferral affect taxable wages?', 'Eligible pretax salary deferrals generally reduce current federal income-tax wages, while employment-tax treatment must be applied separately. The money goes to the retirement plan rather than take-home pay. The annual personal deferral limit and plan deposit rules still apply.','A paycheck directs part of gross compensation to the plan. The diagram separates current cash received from the deferred retirement amount.'),
('How does a Roth 401(k) deferral differ from a pretax deferral?', 'A designated Roth deferral generally uses compensation included in current taxable income. Qualified withdrawal treatment differs from pretax money. Both contribution tracking and payroll reporting must identify the type; choosing Roth does not simply remove the current income tax.','An employee changes the election from pretax to Roth. The drawing keeps the plan deposit but changes the current tax label.'),
('How does a cash balance contribution create a company funding obligation?', 'A cash balance plan is a defined benefit plan with actuarial calculations and ongoing funding requirements. Funding and deductible contribution amounts depend on the plan and census, not a universal percentage of company profit. Review multi-year cash needs and staff costs before adoption.','An owner receives an actuarial proposal. The funding arrow represents a company obligation, not a freely reversible personal savings transfer.'),
('How does employee eligibility change an owner-only retirement plan?', 'Eligible common-law employees can change an owner-only plan\'s participation and testing requirements. Hiring needs a plan review rather than an assumption that the existing solo design continues unchanged. Related employers can also affect who must be considered.','The owner hires staff. The diagram adds an employee plan path to show why the employer picture has changed.'),
('How does S corporation salary support an employer retirement contribution?', 'The shareholder-employee\'s eligible compensation, generally W-2 wages, supports plan calculations under the applicable terms. S corporation distributions do not create compensation for this purpose. Start with reasonable wages and then compute supported plan funding.','The owner increases supported wages for real services. The plan calculation uses eligible compensation rather than total distributions.'),
('How does a SEP employer contribution differ from an employee salary deferral?', 'A SEP generally involves employer contributions under its eligibility and allocation rules; it is not the same mechanism as employee 401(k) salary deferrals. Self-employed contribution calculations can differ from employee percentages. Compare the full staff cost and actual plan terms.','The employer funds a SEP for eligible participants. The drawing uses an employer funding path instead of a paycheck withholding path.'),
('How does owning two companies affect retirement plan coverage?', 'Controlled-group and affiliated-service-group rules can require businesses to be considered together. Separate entity names do not automatically create independent contribution and employee-coverage opportunities. Provide the administrator with complete ownership and service relationships.','An owner has two operating companies with separate payrolls. The retirement review must consider the relationship before treating the plans as unrelated.'),
('How does a business retirement deduction affect cash available for expansion?', 'Funding moves cash out of business liquidity and into the plan, even when a deduction lowers current tax. Compare the net tax benefit with the actual contribution, employee cost and operating reserve. A deductible payment can still reduce cash needed for projects.','The owner considers a large contribution while planning new equipment. The drawing shows both uses competing for the same cash reserve.'),
('How does correcting a retirement contribution require coordination with payroll?', 'The correction depends on whether the problem is an excess deferral, annual addition, employer deduction or operational error. Payroll and plan reporting may both require attention. The administrator and preparer should identify the procedure before an owner makes an ordinary withdrawal.','Two plans together create an excess. The correction route must reconcile the plan records and the compensation reporting.'),
]),
('Business Deductions','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p463','deductions',[
('How does an accountable plan reimbursement move through company books?', 'The employee substantiates a qualifying business cost and returns excess advances; the company records the reimbursement under the applicable rules. Proper treatment can differ from wages. The expense must qualify independently, and a written policy does not make personal spending deductible.','An owner submits receipts for a genuine business expense. The diagram routes reimbursement separately from payroll compensation.'),
('How does a personally paid business expense reach the correct entity?', 'Identify which taxpayer incurred the business obligation, then document reimbursement, contribution or another appropriate arrangement. The payer account alone does not establish deductibility. Avoid recording the same expense on both the individual and entity returns.','A shareholder pays a company invoice personally. The diagram shows documentation and entity reimbursement rather than a second personal deduction.'),
('How does mixed-use vehicle spending become a business deduction?', 'Separate business, commuting and personal use, then apply an eligible expense method consistently. A company payment does not make all miles business-related. Keep logs and receipts sufficient to support the business portion and any payroll benefit treatment.','An owner uses one car for clients and family. The diagram splits use before calculating the deduction.'),
('How does a business meal expense differ from a personal meal?', 'The business purpose and substantiation must support the underlying expense, and applicable deduction limits still apply. Paying with a corporate card does not change a personal meal\'s character. Employee benefit and reimbursement treatment can need separate analysis.','A restaurant bill has a client meeting purpose and attendee record. The diagram keeps qualification ahead of the deduction.'),
('How does travel reimbursement differ from an unrestricted travel allowance?', 'Substantiated qualifying costs under an accountable arrangement can receive different treatment from a nonaccountable allowance. Return excess amounts and document business purpose. If a payment does not meet the accountable rules, wages may be the correct path.','The employer advances travel cash and receives an expense reconciliation. The diagram includes the unused amount returned to the company.'),
('How does depreciation differ from deducting an entire purchase immediately?', 'Depreciation recovers qualifying asset cost under tax rules over time or eligible acceleration provisions. Expensing requires its own eligibility and election analysis. Cash payment, accounting expense and tax deduction can occur on different schedules.','A business buys equipment and places it in service later. The diagram separates purchase cash from the tax recovery schedule.'),
('How does deducting business interest differ from repaying loan principal?', 'Interest on a qualifying business borrowing can have expense treatment, subject to applicable limits and tracing. Principal repayment reduces the debt balance and is generally not a business expense. Split lender payments rather than deducting the entire cash outflow.','A loan payment contains principal and interest. The diagram routes them to the balance sheet and expense review separately.'),
('How does an expense correction affect both business and personal returns?', 'An expense adjustment can change entity income, owner allocations, basis and the household calculation. Verify the appropriate correction procedure and coordinate all affected returns. A business journal entry is not proof that a filed personal return already reflects the change.','A preparer finds a company expense omitted from the books. The diagram traces the correction to the entity result and owner reporting.'),
('How does a home office review connect to a reimbursement arrangement?', 'A home office needs eligible use and supporting records; reimbursement and individual deduction treatment depend on the owner\'s employment and entity facts. Do not assume that working at home makes every household cost a corporate expense. Allocate and substantiate qualifying amounts.','An owner uses a dedicated workspace and pays household bills personally. The diagram shows eligibility and allocation before reimbursement.'),
('How does reimbursing two owners avoid duplicate business deductions?', 'Each submission should identify the actual expense, business purpose and prior payment or reimbursement. One cost should not be reimbursed or deducted twice because both owners attended or used it. Maintain a common approval ledger and reconcile company card charges.','Two partners submit the same conference hotel charge. The diagram places one approved cost ahead of payment to prevent duplication.'),
]),
('Entity Structuring','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/s-corporation-stock-and-debt-basis','loans',[
('How does an owner loan to a business change the balance sheet?', 'A genuine advance creates a business payable and an owner receivable. Principal funding is not customer income or a wage expense. Record terms, actual transfer and repayment; tax basis effects depend on the entity and transaction.','An owner advances cash for operations. The diagram shows matching debt records rather than an immediate deduction.'),
('How does a capital investment differ from an owner loan?', 'Capital is an ownership investment, while a genuine loan creates a repayment obligation. The economic facts and documentation determine treatment. Their basis, return-of-cash and legal consequences differ, so do not alternate labels solely to obtain a desired tax result.','The owner funds expansion and decides whether the business must repay the amount. The diagram presents equity and debt as distinct classifications.'),
('How does reduced debt basis affect an owner loan repayment?', 'An S corporation loss deduction can reduce basis in qualifying shareholder debt. Later repayment can therefore produce taxable income even though it repays an amount originally advanced. Reconcile any basis restoration and apply the appropriate character rules.','The company repays a loan used to support earlier losses. The diagram routes repayment through the adjusted-basis review.'),
('How does a loan guarantee differ from cash advanced by a shareholder?', 'A guarantee commits the owner to possible repayment but generally does not itself create S corporation debt basis. A bona fide direct advance has different facts. Actual payment under a guarantee may require a new analysis when it occurs.','The bank lends to the corporation and the owner signs a guarantee. The diagram keeps the bank loan distinct from a direct owner loan.'),
('How does interest on a shareholder loan reach the owner return?', 'Interest is separate from principal and can create income to the lender, with payer deduction treatment subject to applicable rules. Use supportable terms and review related-party timing and reporting. A payment described as loan interest must reflect a real debt relationship.','The business pays principal and interest on a documented owner loan. The diagram labels the two components differently.'),
('How does stock basis differ from cash in the company bank account?', 'Stock basis is an owner tax calculation based on investment and prescribed adjustments. A company cash balance can result from borrowing, earnings or other events and does not establish shareholder basis. Maintain the basis schedule independently from bank reconciliation.','The company has a large bank balance after borrowing. The diagram shows why borrowed company cash is not automatically owner stock basis.'),
('How does a shareholder contribution affect a later distribution?', 'A genuine capital contribution generally increases stock basis, while a later distribution must be evaluated under the applicable ordering and distribution rules. Actual timing and other income or losses matter. Do not assume a temporary entry without a real transfer creates basis.','The owner contributes cash before a future withdrawal. The diagram connects the actual funding with the basis review.'),
('How does intercompany loan repayment differ from an operating expense?', 'Principal repayment settles a liability; it is not generally a deductible operating expense. Interest and any service charges are different transactions requiring their own support. Reconcile both entities so one company\'s payment matches the other\'s receivable reduction.','Company B repays money borrowed from Company A. The diagram shows settlement on both balance sheets.'),
('How does paying personal bills from a company create tax classification questions?', 'Identify whether the payment is compensation, a distribution, a bona fide loan or another supported item. Personal spending is not an ordinary company expense merely because the company paid. The classification depends on facts, documentation and subsequent conduct.','The company pays an owner\'s personal invoice. The diagram routes it to owner-payment review rather than automatic business deduction.'),
('How does a loan schedule connect to the filed business return?', 'Opening principal, advances, repayments, interest and ending balances should reconcile with the books and supporting agreements. Tax basis can differ from principal. Connect the lender and borrower schedules to avoid mismatched income, expense or shareholder-basis reporting.','The owner and company have different ending loan figures. Reconcile the actual transactions before finalizing either return.'),
]),
('Tax Compliance','https://www.irs.gov/publications/p505','payments',[
('How does an owner estimated tax payment differ from payroll withholding?', 'Estimated payments are made directly toward the owner\'s tax liability, while payroll withholding is taken from compensation and remitted through the employer. Both can credit the return under applicable rules, but timing and reporting differ. Reconcile the two payment sources.','An owner has wages and pass-through profit. The diagram brings withholding and estimated payments into the same household tax reconciliation.'),
('How does a business tax reserve move into an owner payment?', 'A reserve is a cash-planning decision, not a tax deduction. When funds are transferred from an entity to an owner, classify the transfer correctly before the owner pays tax. The eventual estimated payment reduces tax due, not operating profit.','An S corporation sets aside money for shareholder taxes. The diagram separates reserve, distribution and IRS payment.'),
('How does a safe harbor differ from the final income tax calculation?', 'A safe harbor can protect against an underpayment penalty when its requirements are met. The final tax return still calculates actual liability from current facts. A protected payment pattern can leave additional tax due, so budget the remaining amount separately.','The owner pays a prior-year-based amount and earns more this year. The diagram shows a possible remaining balance after the payment credit.'),
('How does annualizing income change estimated tax installments?', 'The annualized income installment method considers uneven income and deductions over the relevant periods. It requires timely records and the applicable calculation, not an informal decision to delay payments. Review withholding and state treatment with the projection.','A business earns most income late in the year. The diagram links dated income periods to the installment calculation.'),
('How does a rental sale trigger a new tax payment projection?', 'A sale can add gain and depreciation-related amounts to the household return. Mortgage payoff affects cash, not the same tax calculation. Update the projected liability and installment needs using adjusted basis, transaction costs and other owner income.','A sale closes after the prior projection was prepared. The diagram adds the sale result before recalculating payment needs.'),
('How does a tax return extension affect payments?', 'An extension generally changes the filing timeline without automatically extending the tax payment deadline. Estimate the remaining balance by the original due date and review each return separately. Interest and penalty treatment depends on the actual payment and filing facts.','The owner waits for a K-1 and extends filing. The diagram keeps the payment obligation on a separate track.'),
('How does a prior-year overpayment become a current-year tax credit?', 'A properly elected carryforward applies an overpayment toward the next year\'s tax, with credit timing determined under applicable rules. Confirm the return election and account records. Do not also treat the same dollars as a cash refund received.','The owner carries a refund forward. The diagram shows a tax credit rather than a new business expense.'),
('How does updating tax projections connect to quarterly bookkeeping?', 'A reliable projection starts with reconciled business results and adds tax adjustments and household items. Book profit, taxable income and available cash are not identical. Updating all three views helps the owner choose payments and implementation actions.','The bookkeeper closes the quarter and the preparer updates the tax estimate. The diagram shows the handoff from books to household liability.'),
('How does spouse withholding cover joint-return business income?', 'On a joint return, household withholding can help cover the combined tax. Review the actual withholding amount, annual rules and other income before reducing estimated payments. A separate company still needs correct reporting even when household wages provide payment coverage.','One spouse has payroll withholding and the other has business profit. The drawing joins payment credits at the joint-return level.'),
('How does a final tax balance differ from deductible business spending?', 'A personal federal income-tax payment generally is not a business deduction. It settles the owner\'s liability after the return computation. Entity-level taxes and other deductible taxes require their own rules, so payment purpose and taxpayer identity matter.','An owner pays the final individual return balance from funds distributed by a business. The diagram does not treat that payment as an operating expense.'),
]),
]
